## Solution

**The answer is NO.** The capacity ordering does *not* guarantee a continuous injection into a finite-radius ball. The obstruction is topological: metric capacity controls only cardinality-at-a-scale, not connectedness.

---

### Counterexample

Let  
- $X = [0,1]$ with the usual metric $d_X(s,t)=|s-t|$ (compact, nonempty),  
- $Y = \mathbb{N}=\{1,2,3,\dots\}$ with the discrete metric $d(m,n)=1$ for $m\neq n$ and $d(m,m)=0$ (nonempty).

Use the standard definition of metric capacity as the packing number
$$\kappa_Z(\varepsilon)=\sup\bigl\{|S|:S\subseteq Z,\ S\text{ is }\varepsilon\text{-separated}\bigr\},\qquad \varepsilon\in(0,1].$$

**Capacity condition holds.** For $X=[0,1]$, any $\varepsilon$-separated set has at most $\lfloor 1/\varepsilon\rfloor+1$ points, so $\kappa_X(\varepsilon)\le 1/\varepsilon+1<\infty$. For $Y=\mathbb{N}$ with the discrete metric, every two distinct points are at distance $1\ge\varepsilon$, so *every* subset of $Y$ is $\varepsilon$-separated; hence $\kappa_Y(\varepsilon)=|\mathbb{N}|=\aleph_0$. Therefore
$$\kappa_X(\varepsilon)\le\aleph_0=\kappa_Y(\varepsilon)\quad\text{for all }\varepsilon\in(0,1].$$

(The same conclusion holds if "metric capacity" is instead interpreted as the covering number: covering $[0,1]$ needs $\asymp 1/\varepsilon$ balls, finite, while covering infinite discrete $\mathbb{N}$ with $\varepsilon$-balls ($\varepsilon<1$) needs $\aleph_0$ balls; the inequality is preserved.)

**No continuous injection into any ball exists.** With the discrete metric, every metric ball in $Y$ is either a singleton or the whole space:
$$B(m_0,r)=\{m:d(m,m_0)<r\}=\begin{cases}\{m_0\},& r\le 1,\\ \mathbb{N},& r>1.\end{cases}$$
(Closed balls behave identically: singleton for $r<1$, all of $\mathbb{N}$ for $r\ge 1$.) In either case the ball is totally disconnected — it is either a single point or a subset of a discrete space.

Suppose $f:X\to B\subseteq Y$ is continuous, where $B$ is some finite-radius ball. Since $X=[0,1]$ is connected, its continuous image $f(X)$ is a connected subset of $B$. But the only connected subsets of a totally disconnected space are singletons, so $f(X)=\{c\}$ for some $c$; i.e. $f$ is constant. A constant map cannot be injective because $|X|>1$.

Thus **no** continuous injection of $X$ into any finite-radius ball in $Y$ exists, despite the capacity ordering $\kappa_X(\varepsilon)\le\kappa_Y(\varepsilon)$ holding for every $\varepsilon\in(0,1]$.

---

### Why the implication fails (structural reason)

Metric capacity is a purely *cardinal*, scale-by-scale quantity: it records how many points can be mutually separated at distance $\ge\varepsilon$. It is blind to the topology of $Y$ — in particular to whether $Y$ is connected, totally disconnected, or has nontrivial components. A continuous injection from a *connected* compact space must land in a connected subset of the target ball; capacity says nothing about whether such connected subsets exist with enough room. The discrete target $Y$ has unlimited capacity at every scale yet contains no nontrivial connected subset, so it admits no continuous injection from $[0,1]$ into any of its balls.

Hence the proposed implication is **false**.

